Plotinus: beauty, intelligibility, and a later Platonism (@greek-plotinus: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @greek-schools-life, #source @greek-source-plotinus, #philosophy @greek-comparative-inquiry, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-comparative-inquiry) { r_375WTA2HXW04F1FC4FVW20B9N9
**Read:** [An Essay on the Beautiful](../sources/On-the-Beautiful-Taylor.lingua#r_7KVSD4FCD7B57PCG677NRSEXBB), translated by Thomas Taylor, a translation of Ennead I.6. Begin with the account of sensible beauty and the problem of explaining it through proportion, then follow the discussion of soul, character, and intelligible beauty. Read Taylor's introduction as a separate interpretation.

**Preparation:** [Symposium's account of love and ascent](../plato/Symposium.lingua#r_5NMHFGWX7QGRJ65XQDFFEG9K8B) and the distinction between an argument and an image. [Republic's comparisons concerning understanding](../plato/Republic.lingua#r_05G1N50PP71GRGDNAPG38CQ8J5) is useful further depth.

Plotinus, a third-century CE philosopher, interprets the Platonic tradition in a later intellectual setting. Ask how the account connects the beauty encountered through the senses with an intelligible order and the condition of the person who apprehends it.

**Practice:** reconstruct the objection to explaining every kind of beauty through the proportions of parts. Then compare one step in the account's movement toward intelligible beauty with Diotima's reported teaching in Symposium. Identify an argument, an analogy, and a claim requiring additional support.

**Check:** defend an interpretation using both texts and explain a genuine difference. This treatise is an entrance into Plotinus' philosophy; distinguish the account established here from a reconstruction of the entire Enneads.

**Finish:** [A comparative philosophical investigation](../questions/Comparative-investigation.lingua#r_20BK69Q76HJXAGZ5J1T097X29G). **Further depth:** [Learning to read Ancient Greek](../languages/Read-original-texts.lingua#r_1Q07KPDNG2JTY7HRWQY5H6T5MJ). **Return:** [Schools of life](Schools-of-life.lingua#r_58ENCWSNQARKCDXEP8FVWY9507) · [Ancient Greek philosophy](../Ancient-Greek-Philosophy.lingua#r_464ZHR7ZYC234A0XX2QEW47VB3).
